WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.509
Batch size tuning is bounded by hardware.

00:00:02.509 --> 00:00:06.145
GPU VRAM is usually the first constraint people notice because model weights,

00:00:06.145 --> 00:00:09.460
runtime buffers, and the KV cache all compete for space.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.430
System RAM matters too, especially when the model is partially offloaded,

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:16.960
when the runtime uses CPU memory for staging, or when the machine falls back to swapping.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.215
Once swapping begins,

00:00:18.215 --> 00:00:21.560
performance can collapse even if the process does not crash immediately.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.885
Memory bandwidth and compute utilization are just as important as raw capacity.

00:00:24.885 --> 00:00:29.734
A GPU with enough VRAM may still be underused if the runtime cannot feed it efficiently.

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:33.495
A CPU-only setup may be limited by memory bandwidth, thread scheduling,

00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:34.465
and cache behavior.

00:00:34.465 --> 00:00:36.715
Integrated GPUs share memory with the system,

00:00:36.715 --> 00:00:40.345
so aggressive settings can affect both inference and the rest of the machine.

00:00:40.345 --> 00:00:44.350
Context length has a direct impact because longer conversations and larger documents

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:45.620
require more attention state.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.940
The KV cache stores intermediate state that lets generation continue without reprocessing

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.540
the entire context every time.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.650
That cache is useful, but it is not free.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:55.884
More concurrent sequences, longer contexts,

00:00:55.884 --> 00:00:59.430
and larger batch-related settings can increase memory pressure quickly.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:03.230
Oversized batch settings can look attractive because they promise more parallelism,

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:06.935
but they can also cause out-of-memory errors, runtime instability, high latency,

00:01:06.935 --> 00:01:07.960
or slower throughput.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.750
The practical goal is to find a setting that keeps the hardware busy without pushing it

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.080
into a fragile state.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.110
Stable headroom matters,

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.890
especially for systems that need to run for hours instead of surviving one benchmark.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.210
This is why a stable configuration usually leaves deliberate headroom.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:25.190
A machine that reports just enough free VRAM during a clean test may fail when the prompt

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:25.750
is longer,

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:29.220
another process opens, or concurrent requests arrive.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.490
Headroom is not wasted capacity.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.140
It is the margin that keeps inference predictable when real operating conditions are less

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.240
tidy than a benchmark.
